# Auto-push to GitHub

After completing an implementation task (code changes, fixes, refactors), **commit and push to GitHub automatically** unless the user explicitly says not to push, or there is nothing to commit.

## Workflow

1. Run `git status` — if there are no changes, skip.
2. Stage relevant files (never commit secrets like `.env`).
3. Write a concise commit message focused on **why**.
4. `git push origin` on the current branch (typically `main`).

## Do not push when

- User says "don't push", "local only", or similar.
- Only exploratory/read-only work with no file changes.
- Commit would fail hooks — fix and create a **new** commit (do not amend unless user rules allow).

## After push

Briefly note the commit hash and that it was pushed — no need to ask "Want this pushed?"
